<h3>After-Hours PPC Strategy</h3>
<p>Emergency restoration searches do not follow business hours. Pipe bursts at midnight. Fires happen on weekends. Your paid advertising must be always-on.</p>
<p><strong>After-hours PPC rules:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Run campaigns 24/7/365</strong> — never pause for nights or weekends</li>
<li><strong>Increase bids during high-risk weather</strong> — storms, freezes, heat waves</li>
<li><strong>Use call-only campaigns</strong> for true emergency keywords — bypass the website entirely</li>
<li><strong>Enable call extensions</strong> on every ad — the phone icon increases CTR by 8% (Google)</li>
<li><strong>Schedule bid adjustments</strong> — increase mobile bids by 25-50% during overnight hours</li>
<li><strong>Create separate ad copy for after-hours</strong> — "24/7 Emergency Response — Call Now"</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Budget allocation:</strong> Emergency restoration PPC typically costs $150-$400 per qualified lead, with CPCs of $25-$120 depending on market competitiveness (PushLeads, 2025). One major water damage job ($5,000-$15,000) pays for months of advertising.</p>
<h3>The "What to Do Now" Content Strategy</h3>
<p>When customers discover damage, they often search for immediate guidance before calling a company. Capturing this search intent builds trust and positions you as the expert.</p>
<p>Create content for these emergency queries:<br />
- "What to do if your basement floods"<br />
- "First steps after a house fire"<br />
- "How to stop water damage from spreading"<br />
- "Is mold dangerous? What to do now"<br />
- "How to file an insurance claim for water damage"</p>
<p>Each piece should:<br />
- Provide genuinely useful immediate steps<br />
- Include a prominent CTA: "Call us now for emergency response"<br />
- Explain why professional restoration is necessary (mold risk, structural damage, insurance requirements)<br />
- End with your 24/7 phone number</p>
<p>This content serves dual purposes: it ranks for informational keywords (building authority) and converts readers into emergency callers (generating revenue).</p>
