<h2>Chapter 4: Website Design That Converts Crisis Visitors</h2>
<h3>The Mobile-First Emergency Experience</h3>
<p>Over 60% of restoration website traffic comes from mobile devices — and during emergencies, that number climbs to 80% (ARMA SEO, 2026). Your website must be built for someone standing in a flooded basement, scrolling with one thumb, under stress.</p>
<p><strong>Mobile optimization requirements:</strong><br />
- Load time under 3 seconds (under 1 second for emergency landing pages)<br />
- Click-to-call button fixed at top of screen<br />
- No pop-ups that block content on mobile<br />
- Large tap targets (buttons at least 44px tall)<br />
- Readable text without zooming (16px minimum)<br />
- No horizontal scrolling</p>
<p>Google's Core Web Vitals now directly impact rankings. Slow-loading restoration websites lose both rankings and customers.</p>
<h3>Emergency CTA Prominence: Above the Fold, Always</h3>
<p>The fold is not a suggestion. It is a conversion threshold.</p>
<p>Every page on your restoration website — especially the homepage and service pages — must display these elements without scrolling:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Phone number in large text</strong> with a click-to-call link</li>
<li><strong>"24/7 Emergency Service" banner</strong> in high-contrast color</li>
<li><strong>Primary CTA button:</strong> "Call Now for Emergency Response" or "Get Help Now"</li>
<li><strong>Response time promise:</strong> "We respond in 60 minutes or less"</li>
<li><strong>Trust signal:</strong> Star rating, review count, or certification badge</li>
</ol>
