<h3>The Four Tiers of Insurance Relationships</h3>
<h4>Tier 1: Insurance Agent Referrals (Easiest Entry)</h4>
<p>There are over 36,000 independent insurance agencies in the U.S. (Insurance Information Institute). Most have no formal restoration partnerships. This is your easiest entry point.</p>
<p><strong>How to build agent relationships:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify local agencies</strong> — Search "insurance agents [city]" and build a target list of 50-100</li>
<li><strong>Drop off educational materials</strong> — Not a sales pitch. A folder with:</li>
<li>Your company brochure</li>
<li>"What to tell clients when they have water damage" one-pager</li>
<li>Your 24/7 emergency card</li>
<li>List of your certifications</li>
<li><strong>Offer co-marketing</strong> — "Refer your clients to us, and we will feature your agency in our blog content"</li>
<li><strong>Host lunch-and-learns</strong> — "How to help clients navigate water damage claims"</li>
<li><strong>Follow up quarterly</strong> — Not with a sales ask. With useful information: seasonal risks, new regulations, claim trends</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Key insight:</strong> Insurance agents want to look knowledgeable to their clients. If you make them the hero who "knows a great restoration company," they will refer you consistently.</p>
<h4>Tier 2: Independent Adjuster Relationships</h4>
<p>Independent adjusters (IAs) handle claims for multiple carriers. They choose which restoration company to recommend or approve.</p>
<p><strong>How to build adjuster relationships:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get on their vendor list</strong> — Ask every adjuster you meet: "How do I get on your approved vendor list?"</li>
<li><strong>Deliver flawless documentation</strong> — Xactimate estimates, moisture logs, photo documentation, drying records</li>
<li><strong>Meet response time SLAs</strong> — 4-hour contact, 24-hour on-site, 48-hour scope submission</li>
<li><strong>Be easy to work with</strong> — Answer your phone. Respond to emails same day. Do not argue over line items without justification</li>
<li><strong>Follow up after every job</strong> — "How was the claims process? Anything I can improve?"</li>
<li><strong>Attend adjuster events</strong> — NACA (National Association of Catastrophe Adjusters), regional adjuster associations</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Critical requirement:</strong> Xactimate proficiency. If your estimates do not match adjuster format and line-item expectations, you create friction. Friction kills relationships.</p>
<h4>Tier 3: Third-Party Administrator (TPA) Networks</h4>
<p>TPAs manage preferred vendor programs for multiple insurance carriers. Major TPA networks include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Contractor Connection</strong> (Sedgwick)</li>
<li><strong>Crawford &amp; Company</strong></li>
<li><strong>Alacrity Services</strong></li>
<li><strong>BELFOR</strong> (franchise network with carrier relationships)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>TPA application requirements:</strong><br />
- IICRC Certified Firm status<br />
- WRT-certified technicians minimum (ASD preferred)<br />
- $1M+ general liability, workers comp, auto, umbrella<br />
- Background checks for all technicians<br />
- 24/7 response capability with documented SLAs<br />
- Xactimate proficiency and direct billing<br />
- Clean complaint history<br />
- Geographic coverage matching TPA needs</p>
