<h3>Weather-Triggered Advertising</h3>
<p>The most sophisticated restoration companies use weather data to trigger advertising automatically.</p>
<p><strong>How weather-triggered ads work:</strong><br />
1. Connect weather APIs (OpenWeatherMap, Weather Unlocked) to your ad platform<br />
2. Define trigger conditions:<br />
   - Rainfall exceeding 2 inches in 24 hours<br />
   - Temperature dropping below 20°F (frozen pipe risk)<br />
   - Storm warnings issued<br />
   - Humidity exceeding 80% for 48+ hours (mold risk)<br />
3. Create automated rules:<br />
   - Increase bids by 50% when triggers activate<br />
   - Swap to emergency ad copy<br />
   - Activate display campaigns in affected zip codes<br />
   - Send SMS alerts to your team</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> When the National Weather Service issues a freeze warning for your service area, your Google Ads automatically increase bids on "frozen pipe water damage" keywords, your ad copy switches to "Frozen Pipe Emergency — 24/7 Response," and your display ads geofence the affected neighborhoods.</p>
<p>Agencies like Remediation Restoration Marketing and Mad Fish Digital specialize in weather-triggered restoration campaigns.</p>
<h3>Geofencing Disaster Areas</h3>
<p>Geofencing creates virtual perimeters around physical locations. When a mobile device enters the perimeter, the user becomes eligible for your ads.</p>
<p><strong>Geofencing applications for restoration:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Disaster zone targeting</strong> — Create geofences around neighborhoods affected by floods, fires, or storms</li>
<li><strong>Competitor conquesting</strong> — Target ads to users near competitor offices or job sites</li>
<li><strong>Property manager targeting</strong> — Geofence commercial property management conferences and trade shows</li>
<li><strong>Insurance agent targeting</strong> — Geofence local insurance agency clusters</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout callout-tip">
<p><strong>Best practices:</strong><br />
- Keep geofences tight (0.5-1 mile radius) to avoid wasted spend<br />
- Combine geofencing with weather triggers for maximum relevance<br />
- Use different creative for disaster zones vs. general targeting<br />
- Set frequency caps to avoid annoying affected residents</p>
</div>
<h3>Retargeting Website Visitors</h3>
<p>Only 2-5% of website visitors convert on their first visit. Retargeting captures the other 95-98%.</p>
<p><strong>Retargeting strategy for restoration:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Install retargeting pixels</strong> — Google Ads, Facebook, Microsoft Advertising</li>
<li><strong>Create audience segments:</strong></li>
<li>All website visitors (broad)</li>
<li>Emergency landing page visitors (high intent)</li>
<li>Visitors who spent 60+ seconds on site (engaged)</li>
<li>Visitors who clicked to call but did not complete (warm lead)</li>
<li><strong>Ad creative by segment:</strong></li>
<li>Broad: Brand awareness + 24/7 messaging</li>
<li>High intent: "Still need help? Call now" + response time promise</li>
<li>Engaged: Customer testimonials + before/after photos</li>
<li>Warm lead: "You called but did not reach us. Try again — we are here 24/7"</li>
<li><strong>Frequency cap:</strong> 3-5 impressions per day maximum</li>
</ol>
